(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-pericardial-disease-vignettes"]={"title":"Pericardial Disease Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 5","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 27-year-old woman recovering well from her first episode of acute viral pericarditis asks how likely it is to come back. Which estimate is most accurate?","choices":["About 60%","It never recurs","About 15 to 30%","Under 1%"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Recurrent pericarditis affects about 15 to 30% of patients after acute pericarditis, which is why completing the full course and recognizing symptoms early matter. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man with advanced constrictive pericarditis and moderate biatrial enlargement reports new palpitations. His pulse is irregularly irregular. Which rhythm is he most prone to?","choices":["Atrial flutter with 2:1 block","Complete heart block","Ventricular tachycardia","Atrial fibrillation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Atrial fibrillation is common in advanced constrictive pericarditis, encouraged by biatrial enlargement; nonspecific ST and T-wave changes, tachycardia and low voltage are the other findings. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 48-year-old man becomes hypotensive and short of breath an hour after a catheter procedure perforates his heart. Echocardiogram shows only a small pericardial effusion, estimated at about 150 mL, with right ventricular diastolic collapse. Which best explains why so little fluid has compromised him?","choices":["The echocardiogram underestimated a liter","The pericardium was already calcified","Rapid filling of an unstretched sac","A small effusion is always harmless"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Acute effusions can produce tamponade with small amounts of fluid (under 200 mL) because the pericardium has no time to stretch, unlike chronic effusions that grow large over time. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 30-year-old man is diagnosed with acute idiopathic pericarditis after a viral illness. He is afebrile, his troponin is normal, the echocardiogram shows no effusion, and he has no contraindication to any medication. Which treatment is most appropriate?","choices":["Prednisone plus colchicine","Anakinra plus colchicine","Vancomycin plus ceftriaxone","Ibuprofen plus colchicine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug such as ibuprofen plus colchicine is the initial recommended therapy for most acute idiopathic or viral pericarditis, with gastrointestinal protection. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man has a story strongly suggesting constrictive pericarditis: edema, ascites, an elevated jugular venous pressure with the Kussmaul sign (a rise on inspiration). His chest radiograph shows no pericardial calcification. How should this radiograph be interpreted?","choices":["It does not exclude constriction","It rules out constrictive disease","It points to restrictive myopathy","It shows the cause is tamponade"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pericardial calcification is highly consistent with constriction when present, but most patients do not have it on chest radiograph, so a film without calcium cannot rule the diagnosis out. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 55-year-old woman with metastatic lung cancer has 2 days of worsening dyspnea and chest discomfort. Blood pressure is 84/56, heart rate 122, jugular veins are distended, and heart sounds are muffled. Her systolic pressure falls by 18 mmHg on inspiration (pulsus paradoxus, a systolic fall of more than 10 mmHg). Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Tension pneumothorax","Cardiac tamponade","Constrictive pericarditis","Acute pericarditis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Hypotension, distended jugular veins and muffled heart sounds (the Beck triad) with pulsus paradoxus and tachycardia in a patient with malignancy are cardiac tamponade, pericardial fluid under pressure compressing the heart. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman with a large effusion is hypotensive with muffled heart sounds. A transthoracic echocardiogram is performed at the bedside. Which finding would indicate that the effusion is causing tamponade?","choices":["Right ventricular diastolic collapse","A heart swinging within the fluid","Septal bounce toward the left","Thickened pericardial layers"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Right ventricular diastolic collapse is evident with cardiac tamponade and absent with a pericardial effusion alone; echocardiography assesses the hemodynamic significance of the fluid. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman treated for breast cancer 2 years ago has 3 weeks of fatigue and exertional dyspnea. Her blood pressure is 128/80 without hypotension. The chest radiograph shows an enlarged, water-bottle (globular) cardiac silhouette with clear lung fields. Which test is required to confirm a pericardial effusion?","choices":["Repeat chest radiograph","Twelve-lead electrocardiogram","Coronary angiography","Transthoracic echocardiogram"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Clinical evaluation, electrocardiogram and chest radiograph may suggest a pericardial effusion, but an echocardiogram is required to confirm it and to assess its hemodynamic significance. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"A 41-year-old woman is being treated for acute pericarditis with an anti-inflammatory and colchicine. She asks whether she may take brisk walks while recovering. Which heart rate ceiling should guide her activity?","choices":["No limit if she feels well","Under 100 beats per minute","Under 60 beats per minute","Under 150 beats per minute"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. During the restriction, strenuous activity is avoided and the maximal heart rate is kept under 100 beats per minute, for at least 1 month and until symptoms and C-reactive protein normalize. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with ovarian cancer has had three drainages for a recurrent pericardial effusion, and the latest echocardiogram shows a loculated collection behind the heart. Which procedure is most appropriate?","choices":["Pericardial window","Fourth needle tap","Thoracentesis","Diuretic therapy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A pericardiectomy or pericardial window is performed for recurrent effusions and for loculated effusions, especially posterior ones that cannot be approached percutaneously. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman developed early signs of constriction 6 weeks after an episode of acute pericarditis. She is hemodynamically stable, and cardiac magnetic resonance shows pericardial edema with late gadolinium enhancement. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Observation with no treatment","A medical trial with diuretics","Pericardiectomy within the week","Urgent pericardiocentesis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Early, hemodynamically stable constriction may get a trial of the acute pericarditis medicines for longer (2 to 3 months) with diuretics; edema or late enhancement on cardiac magnetic resonance suggests reversible inflammation. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man is 4 days into his recovery from a large anterior myocardial infarction when he suddenly becomes hypotensive, with distended neck veins and muffled heart sounds. Which is the most likely explanation?","choices":["Free-wall rupture with tamponade","Dressler syndrome","Acute mitral stenosis","Constrictive pericarditis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Free-wall rupture after myocardial infarction is a recognized cause of tamponade, alongside acute pericarditis, iatrogenic injury, trauma, malignancy and aortic dissection. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man on dialysis has missed several sessions. He has a large pericardial effusion with stable blood pressure, and his blood urea nitrogen is 112 mg/dL. Which treatment addresses the cause?","choices":["Pericardiectomy","Dialysis","Antituberculous therapy","Corticosteroids"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Treating the underlying disorder is key; uremic pericarditis, associated with a blood urea nitrogen above 60 mg/dL, is treated with dialysis, and further management follows the cause. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman with metastatic lung cancer has a symptomatic malignant pericardial effusion that has already reaccumulated once after drainage. Which consult is recommended to improve her outcomes and quality of life?","choices":["Rheumatology","Endocrinology","Palliative care","Physical therapy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Symptomatic malignant pericardial effusion carries a limited life expectancy (median 2 to 4 months) and often recurs; a palliative care consult is recommended to improve outcomes and quality of life. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with acute pericarditis is starting ibuprofen and colchicine. He has a history of dyspepsia. Which addition to his regimen is most appropriate?","choices":["A proton pump inhibitor","An oral anticoagulant","A prophylactic antibiotic","A loop diuretic"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Gastrointestinal protection is provided with anti-inflammatory therapy, for example a proton pump inhibitor, because these drugs reduce the stomach's protective prostaglandins. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 48-year-old woman is discharged on ibuprofen and colchicine for acute pericarditis. Which new symptom should prompt her to go straight to the emergency department rather than call for an appointment?","choices":["Lightheadedness with shortness of breath","Mild fatigue in the late afternoon","Pain that still eases on leaning forward","Mild indigestion after doses of ibuprofen"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Red-flag symptoms for tamponade or worsening disease, such as dyspnea, lightheadedness or falling exercise tolerance from low cardiac output, need emergency evaluation rather than a routine visit. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man with acute pericarditis has taken ibuprofen and colchicine for 2 weeks. He has been symptom-free for 4 days, and his weekly C-reactive protein has just returned to normal. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Begin tapering the anti-inflammatory","Stop every medication today","Increase the anti-inflammatory","Switch over to prednisone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. With the C-reactive protein approach, tapering starts once the patient is symptom-free and the level has normalized; colchicine continues for about 3 months. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"An 81-year-old man with 3 months of weight loss presents with dyspnea and a large pericardial effusion. He has had no recent viral illness, chest surgery or radiation. Which etiology should be most strongly suspected?","choices":["Dressler syndrome","Rheumatic fever","Malignancy","Coxsackievirus"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In older adults there is a higher index of suspicion for malignancy, especially with an effusion or tamponade, and his weight loss without a viral prodrome reinforces it. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man with a large pericardial effusion from metastatic cancer reports a hoarse voice, hiccups and difficulty swallowing. Which mechanism explains his hoarseness?","choices":["Hypoglossal nerve compression","Superior vena cava obstruction","Recurrent laryngeal nerve impingement","Direct invasion of the vocal cords"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Large effusions can cause dysphagia, nausea, hiccups, cough and hoarseness, the hoarseness from impingement of the recurrent laryngeal nerve. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man has had 10 days of gradually worsening pleuritic (worse with a deep breath) chest pain without a clear onset. His temperature is 38.7 degrees Celsius (101.7 degrees Fahrenheit), and the echocardiogram shows a moderate pericardial effusion without collapse of any chamber. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Outpatient ibuprofen and colchicine","Reassurance and repeat echo in 3 months","Immediate pericardiectomy","Hospital admission for workup"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Fever above 38 degrees Celsius, a subacute course over days to weeks and a moderate to large effusion are all high-risk features that favor hospitalization and further evaluation. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man comes to a primary care office with dyspnea, lightheadedness, distended neck veins and a blood pressure of 86/58. He was diagnosed with a pericardial effusion last month. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Schedule cardiology in two weeks","Send him to the emergency department","Order an outpatient echocardiogram","Start ibuprofen and colchicine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Evidence suggesting cardiac tamponade is a high-risk feature that requires hospitalization, and tamponade is an emergency, so he goes to the emergency department now. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 38-year-old woman has her second recurrence of idiopathic pericarditis despite colchicine. Her temperature is 38.4 degrees Celsius, C-reactive protein is 45 mg/L, and cardiac magnetic resonance shows pericardial edema with late gadolinium enhancement. Which therapy is now preferred over corticosteroids?","choices":["Prednisone","Azathioprine","Pericardiectomy","Anakinra"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. With an inflammatory phenotype (C-reactive protein above 10 mg/L, fever, imaging inflammation) and colchicine-resistant recurrence, an anti-interleukin-1 agent such as anakinra or rilonacept is preferred over corticosteroids. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man received chest radiation for lymphoma 20 years ago. He has 8 months of exertional dyspnea, fatigue, leg edema and ascites. His jugular venous pressure is elevated and shows the Kussmaul sign (it rises on inspiration instead of falling), and a pericardial knock (an early diastolic sound, slightly earlier than a third heart sound) is heard. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Infective endocarditis","Constrictive pericarditis","Acute pericarditis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Fluid overload with low output, an elevated jugular venous pressure with the Kussmaul sign and a pericardial knock after prior radiation are the picture of constrictive pericarditis, scarring that limits filling. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man with known malignant pericardial effusion has a blood pressure of 82/50, distended neck veins, muffled heart sounds and pulsus paradoxus (an inspiratory systolic fall of 16 mmHg). Echocardiogram shows a large effusion with right ventricular diastolic collapse. Which is the definitive treatment?","choices":["Loop diuretic infusion","Expedient pericardiocentesis","Intravenous fluids alone","High-dose anti-inflammatories"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Expedient pericardiocentesis is the definitive treatment for cardiac tamponade; it relieves the pressure and the fluid is sent for diagnostic studies. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"A 60-year-old woman with confirmed constrictive pericarditis has failed a 3-month trial of anti-inflammatories and diuretics and remains fluid overloaded. Which service performs the definitive procedure she needs?","choices":["Cardiothoracic surgery","Interventional radiology","Gastroenterology","Nephrology"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Constriction refractory to medical therapy needs pericardiectomy, an operation performed by cardiothoracic surgery working with cardiology. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 79-year-old woman with chronic kidney disease and a prior bleeding stomach ulcer is diagnosed with acute pericarditis. Which medication calls for particular caution in her because it appears on the Beers list?","choices":["Acetaminophen","Omeprazole","Ibuprofen","Pantoprazole"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs such as ibuprofen are on the Beers list and call for caution in older adults, especially with kidney disease or bleeding history; colchicine may need a dose reduction. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 67-year-old former smoker with weight loss has a large pericardial effusion. Lung cancer is suspected, and pericardiocentesis is performed. Which study of the pericardial fluid best addresses this suspicion?","choices":["Gram stain","Fungal culture","Cell count","Cytology"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pericardial fluid is sent for cell count, Gram stain, cultures, acid-fast studies and cytology, and cytology is the study that identifies malignant cells. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"A 37-year-old man was treated for acute pericarditis 4 weeks ago and now feels well. A repeat electrocardiogram shows widespread T-wave inversions without ST elevation, and troponin is normal. Which best explains the tracing?","choices":["Hyperkalemia from colchicine","A later stage of pericarditis","A new silent myocardial infarction","Developing cardiac tamponade"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The classic evolution is widespread ST elevation with PR depression, then ST normalization with T-wave flattening, then inversion of the flattened T waves, and finally a normal tracing. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 40-year-old woman with right lower lobe pneumonia has sharp chest pain. A rubbing sound is heard over the lower chest that is present only while she breathes in and vanishes when she holds her breath. Which is the most likely source of the sound?","choices":["The chest wall","The pleura","The pericardium","The mitral valve"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A pleural rub is tied to respiration, heard in the inspiratory phase, and disappears with breath-holding; a pericardial rub persists, and even intensifies, while the breath is held. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 36-year-old woman has had three episodes of pericarditis in 18 months, each treated in primary care with an anti-inflammatory and colchicine. Which referral is most appropriate?","choices":["Infectious disease","Gastroenterology","Cardiothoracic surgery","Cardiology"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Referral to cardiology is advised for recurrences (recurrent pericarditis), for no response to conservative management and for the development of constrictive pericarditis. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 33"}]};
